## Context
Child of 1131 (oversized skills). `skills/roar/SKILL.md` is 27 KB and does at least five jobs with different triggers and costs: reflect, capture significant project experiences, sweep the codebase for the same anti-pattern, close the ticket and verify its close claims, clean up the completed task. Its memory capture overlaps `hoard` and `memory-sweep`. Author decision 2026-10-20: split it. Skill names stay dragon-themed.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Read-only proposal first: the boundary (what stays in `roar`, what moves to runbooks under `skills/roar/runbooks/`) with a size estimate per file; the author confirmed the revised design on 2026-10-20 (rule: only user-facing skills are skills; internals are scripts > runbooks > reference)
- [ ] `roar` keeps the post-task close-and-clean path: pre-check, close, close-claim check, tracking-ticket check, exit worktree, own-task cleanup, report
- [ ] Memory capture and the similar-pattern sweep live outside roar's SKILL.md as runbooks, each with a one-line when-to-use header, and roar's steps point at them
- [ ] Callers of `roar` (raid, hunt, merge, lair, tests) still reach what they relied on; grep proves it
- [ ] `make skills-catalog` run; `make check-skills-drift` clean
- [ ] No retirement of any skill; `roar` shrinks by more than half
